<!--  how can I add steering to my model?

To implement steering in ROS 2 and Gazebo for an Ackermann vehicle (car-like steering), you need two things: joint structure in your URDF/Xacro and a ROS 2 control / Gazebo plugin to drive those joints.
Step 1: Set Up the Steering Joints in URDF/Xacro

Ackermann steering requires dedicated steer kingpin/pivot joints for the front wheels.

Instead of attaching front wheels directly to the chassis, attach them to intermediate steering knuckles using revolute joints that rotate around the Z-axis (yaw).

<Sequence>
<Step subtitle="URDF / Xacro modification" title="Define Steering Knuckles">
Create left and right steering knuckles connected to your chassis via revolute joints that pivot around the Z-axis:
XML

<!-- Left Steering Joint (Pivot around Z-axis) --
<joint name="fl_steering_joint" type="revolute">
  <parent link="chassis"/>
  <child link="fl_steering_link"/>
  <origin xyz="0.25 0.15 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
  <axis xyz="0 0 1"/>
  <limit lower="-0.6" upper="0.6" effort="10.0" velocity="1.0"/>
</joint>

<link name="fl_steering_link">
  <inertial>
    <mass value="0.1"/>
    <inertia ixx="0.001" ixy="0" ixz="0" iyy="0.001" iyz="0" izz="0.001"/>
  </inertial>
</link>

</Step>

<Step title="Attach Front Wheel to Steering Link">
Attach your front wheel link to the steering link (not the chassis) using a continuous joint around the Y-axis (pitch for rolling):
XML

<!-- Left Front Wheel (Rolls around Y-axis) --
<joint name="fl_wheel_joint" type="continuous">
  <parent link="fl_steering_link"/>
  <child link="fl_wheel_link"/>
  <origin xyz="0 0.05 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
  <axis xyz="0 1 0"/>
</joint>

</Step>

<Step subtitle="Gazebo Sim Integration" title="Add Ackermann Plugin for Gazebo">
Add the Ackermann Drive plugin inside your Gazebo xacro file (e.g., sensors_gazebo.xacro or mobile_robot_gazebo.xacro). This plugin handles front steering angle calculations automatically.
XML

<gazebo>
  <plugin name="gazebo_ros_ackermann_drive" filename="libgazebo_ros_ackermann_drive.so">
    <ros>
      <namespace>/</namespace>
      <remapping>cmd_vel:=cmd_vel</remapping>
      <remapping>odom:=odom</remapping>
    </ros>

    <update_rate">100.0</update_rate>

    <!-- Steering Joints --
    <front_left_joint>fl_wheel_joint</front_left_joint>
    <front_right_joint>fr_wheel_joint</front_right_joint>
    <back_left_joint>rl_wheel_joint</back_left_joint>
    <back_right_joint>rr_wheel_joint</back_right_joint>
    <front_left_steering_joint>fl_steering_joint</front_left_steering_joint>
    <front_right_steering_joint>fr_steering_joint</front_right_steering_joint>

    <!-- Vehicle Dimensions --
    <kingpin_separation>0.3</kingpin_separation>
    <wheelbase>0.5</wheelbase>
    <wheel_diameter>0.15</wheel_diameter>

    <!-- Limits --
    <max_steer>0.6</max_steer>
    <max_wheel_torque>20</max_wheel_torque>
    <max_wheel_acceleration>1.0</max_wheel_acceleration>
  </plugin>
</gazebo>

</Step>
</Sequence>
Step 2: Test Steering Control

After building your workspace (colcon build), launch your robot in Gazebo and drive it using teleop_twist_keyboard or command velocity topics:
Bash

ros2 run teleop_twist_keyboard teleop_twist_keyboard -->